{# Shared TLS snippet — the full `tls { ... }` directive, imported by a
   LITERAL (non-glob) path from:
     - the `*.{{ fleet_domain }}` site block (Caddyfile.j2)
     - every fleet-owned named-port snippet, IF ITS OWN import already
       exists on disk (fleet.core.caddyports)
   A literal import is a hard Caddy validate/reload error if this file is
   missing, which is intentional for the *.{{ fleet_domain }} site — Ansible
   (this role) always renders it before deploying the Caddyfile, in the
   same run, so it exists by the time that import is ever reached.

   Named-port snippets are more cautious: `fleet.core.caddyports.
   render_port_snippet()` only emits the `import` once this file already
   exists on disk; on a host whose `caddy` role hasn't been re-run yet
   (e.g. right after a plain code-only rollout — `push-product`'s git-pull +
   `systemctl restart fleet`, no Ansible involved — reconciles port
   snippets on daemon startup), it inlines the legacy `tls { on_demand }`
   block instead, so an un-migrated on_demand server never ends up
   importing a file that doesn't exist yet. Once this role HAS run and
   created this file, the next `fleet refresh-ports` (or daemon restart)
   flips existing snippets over to the import — see docs/installation.md
   "Switching TLS mode".

   Rendered per `fleet_tls_mode` (group_vars/all.yml; asserted to be
   `on_demand` or `ovh_dns` before this template is ever rendered — see
   roles/caddy/tasks/main.yml). MUST stay multi-line: a single-line
   `tls { on_demand }` is invalid Caddyfile syntax (proven against a real
   `caddy validate` v2.11, 2026-07-25 — "Unexpected next token after '{' on
   same line").

   In ovh_dns mode this is the ONE wildcard cert (*.{{ fleet_domain }})
   shared by the :443 site and every named-port site — same cert name in
   Caddy's storage, since they all import this identical block. #}
{#
   `{env.OVH_*}` are RUNTIME placeholders, resolved by the caddy-dns/ovh
   module's Provision() from caddy.service's environment (EnvironmentFile
   /etc/caddy/ovh.env). Do NOT switch to parse-time `{$OVH_*}`: those are
   substituted while the Caddyfile is read, so every `caddy validate` —
   including the fleet daemon's own, run as the `fleet` user, which can't
   read ovh.env — would see empty values and fail. This file holds no
   secrets, so it is world-readable within its fleet:caddy 2750 directory.
#}
{% if fleet_tls_mode == 'ovh_dns' %}
tls {
    dns ovh {
        endpoint {env.OVH_ENDPOINT}
        application_key {env.OVH_APPLICATION_KEY}
        application_secret {env.OVH_APPLICATION_SECRET}
        consumer_key {env.OVH_CONSUMER_KEY}
    }
}
{% else %}
tls {
    on_demand
}
{% endif %}
